diagnosis_text:
A thorough examination of every layer of the CS-lab-to-Server-Room path reveals no network fault. All three CS-lab hosts tested (cs_hod, cs_lab1, cs_lab4, all on 192.168.2.0/24) reach the Server Room host pc1 (1.0.0.5/8) with 0 % packet loss and ~2 ms RTT. A traceroute confirms the full four-hop data plane: cs_lab machine → router0 (192.168.2.1) → router1 (10.10.0.2) → router2 (20.20.0.2) → pc1.

The RIP routing fabric is correctly converged on all three routers. Router0 carries `R 1.0.0.0/8 [120/3] via 10.10.0.2` (the Server Room prefix); router2 carries `R 192.168.2.0/24 [120/3] via 20.20.0.1` (the CS-lab prefix). Every intermediate router holds consistent, loop-free routes with correct hop counts. All WAN and LAN interfaces are UP with the addresses matching the startup configuration. No iptables or nftables rules exist on any router or on pc1 itself that could block traffic. The ripd daemons on all three routers have been running continuously since lab start (03:17:17) with no mid-session restarts, confirming no transient RIP blackout occurred during the observation window.

Root cause: There is no network-layer fault. The described symptom — CS lab unable to reach the Server Room — does not correspond to any real defect in the current lab state. The ticket's premise is false; the routing and forwarding infrastructure is fully operational.

Corrective action: No network change is required. The investigation should focus on application-layer issues on pc1 (e.g., whether file-sharing services are actually running), or the reported outage may have been a transient client-side issue unrelated to the network.
=== END ===